# Example Caddy config for terminating real HTTPS/WSS in front of the real-time multiplayer WS
# service — same pattern as backend/api/Caddyfile.example, on its own subdomain since the
# frontend connects to it directly via VITE_WS_URL (a full ws(s):// URL, not a path under the
# API's domain — see frontend/src/lib/multiplayer/useMultiplayerRace.ts). Not included as a
# service in docker-compose.prod.yml by default since it needs a real domain name pointed at
# this host to issue a certificate for — meaningless in a local/test environment, and different
# for every real deployment.
#
# Usage once you have a domain pointed at this host:
#   1. Copy this file to Caddyfile (or append this block to the one already serving the api
#      service — Caddy happily serves multiple site blocks from one Caddyfile/one `caddy run`).
#   2. Replace ws.example.com with your real subdomain.
#   3. Install Caddy on the host if it isn't already (see backend/api/Caddyfile.example), or add
#      it as its own compose service if you'd rather keep everything containerized.
#   4. sudo caddy run --config Caddyfile
#
# No special WebSocket directive needed — Caddy's reverse_proxy detects the Connection: Upgrade
# header and proxies the upgraded connection automatically, the same as any other request.
#
# This assumes the ws service is reachable at 127.0.0.1:8081 — which is exactly how
# docker-compose.prod.yml publishes it (bound to localhost only, not the public interface).

ws.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081
}
